  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Federal contract obligations in Arkansas for FY2026 to date total $638M. Award activity so far shows 13,373 awards, with an average award size of $47.7K. Obligations fell 49.5% year over year. Historical yearly obligations provide context: FY21 was $1.5B, FY22 was $1.4B, FY23 was $1.3B, FY24 was $1.9B, FY25 was $1.3B, and FY26 is $638M. The Department of Defense accounts for the largest share of buying agency obligations at $396M, followed by the Department of Veterans Affairs at $92.3M and the Department of State at $32.2M. On the vendor side, BL HARBERT INTERNATIONAL LLC leads with $102M in obligations, and ALBERICI CONSTRUCTORS INC follows with $68.2M. Construction and heavy civil work dominate the industry mix, led by Commercial and Institutional Building Construction at $156M and Other Heavy and Civil Engineering Construction at $117M. These figures are contract obligations; current-year totals are partial (fiscal year to date).",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "What happened to federal contracting activity in Arkansas this fiscal year?",
        "body": "Obligations fell 49.5% year over year. Total FY2026 to date obligations are $638M, compared with prior fiscal years that ranged from $1.3B to $1.9B. Award volume remains substantial at 13,373 awards, but the average award size is $47.7K, reflecting smaller per-award obligations so far."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies, vendors, and industries are largest in Arkansas federal contracting?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense is the largest buying agency with $396M in obligations, followed by the Department of Veterans Affairs at $92.3M and the Department of State at $32.2M. BL HARBERT INTERNATIONAL LLC is the top vendor with $102M, and ALBERICI CONSTRUCTORS INC is second with $68.2M. Industry spending is concentrated in construction: Commercial and Institutional Building Construction is $156M and Other Heavy and Civil Engineering Construction is $117M."
      }
    ]
  },
